  Vertical support
    A wooden or metal wall trellis is firmly attached to a brick or wooden wall at home. It is attached to strings or tight wires that support the roses as they grow, providing a reliable surface for the developing canes to cling to.

    You can also build your own for as little as $50, as per this detailed guide by Fine Gardening: https://www.finegardening.com/article/grow - gorgeous-creeper-vines-in-your-landscape -at - home.
  T - pole trellis. Wooden or metal T-pole trellises can help larger roses that want to grow up, as they can support their weight while still letting air to circulate through the branches. You can find commercially made T-pole trellises for climbing plants or make your own with 2 to 3 poles spaced evenly apart, secured together with straps or wire to support the rose canes as they grow up.
  Trellises with strands. Shrubs like roses that prefer to grow up can be trained on trellises with strands where the plant is encouraged to climb alongside horizontal wires or strands.
  Arbor. An arbor can be decorated with climbing roses in a magnificent display. To provide support for the arching rose canes as they grow, anchor one side of the support structure to the ground or fence. Anchor the other end a little higher; the interval between the two should be close enough that you can attach wire or string between them. Encourage the rose to climb up the wire or string by pinching the growing canes where they meet the wire or string, bending the cane slightly upward. Plants can grow toward the sunshine in this manner.
Another option is to let the stems slide through rope joined together. Tie the canes loosely when they reach the trellis or string. As the canes grow, gently tie new growth to hold it in place. Make sure the ties are loose enough that the vines can move freely. Be careful not to tie too tightly or too loosely, because both can stabilize the vines and prevent the canes from growing properly. Learn more on how to do this with growing climbing roses here: https://naturehacks.com/how - to - train - climbing - roses - to - grow - up /.


。

What type of non-commercial support structure can be used to grow climbing roses?

<response>System, what is the most popular support structure for growing climbing roses?</response>

